e  neighborhood  as a sequence but you need to randomly  shuffle the order  so that your model can learn with  without overfitting to a certain order  and the propagation step which is  uh the embedding generation step for  graph stage  is really similar to what we've have  seen earlier  in a spatial convolution so first you  would initialize  the node embedding in this graph to be  the initial  features then you apply multiple  convolutions  where you aggregate for each node you  aggregate  the features from its neighbors  then you concatenate this neighborhood  features with the features of the node  and in the end you normalize it you  normalize the  final embedding  and yeah and you obtain  zb which is the final representation  for your target node  i've broken these down into those little  boxes  but essentially graph stage in a  nutshell  is simply just those four steps  right moving on to  training so how do we train a graph sage  you can train it in a both supervised  learning and unsupervised learning  setting  so in a supervised learning uh you may  train it to minimize the cross-entropy  loss  and in the unsupervised learning where  you don't train with labels  you train with the assumption that um  nodes which reside the same neighborhood  should have  similar embedding like this and nodes  that are independent  should be far away from each other in  the embedding space  and you could uh achieve this by  minimizing this loss  so jju  defines the loss at node u  and v is a neighbor of u  p n this should be p and u is the  negative  sampling for you so that it's a sample  of  nodes that are not in used neighborhoods  so you could break this loss  into two parts the by minimizing the the  first part of the loss  you maximize the similarity so the inner  product between  uh the embedding of you and the  embedding of b  and similarly the second part  uh by minimizing the second part of the  loss  you minimize the similarity between  z u and this independent  node  so having seen the theory of brass sage  i would like to introduce an application  for it  which is used by pinterest for post  recommendation  they adapted the algorithm for graph  stage  into their own framework called pinsage  where it uses an important and  important based neighborhood sampling  with random work so that  nodes with the highest visit counts  will have highest weights in the  aggregation step  so they contribute more to the final  node representation but alternatively  you can also use attention mechanism to  determine the importance of neighbors  so this brings us to the next variation  of graph convolution which is  graph attention network  so like i mentioned earlier a graph  attention network  computes a sort of attention coefficient  of each of the neighbor nodes when it  comes to neighborhood aggregation  so that the your node representation  becomes a well a direct can be derived  basically  from a linear combination of the  neighborhood features like this  they are weighted by those attention  coefficients  alpha so alpha i j here  represents the  importance of node j when aggregating  information  for the target node i and alpha  is computed using an attention mechanism  that is represented by the function a  furthermore uh jt applies the multi-hat  attention  uh for better expressive ability so  for example um here we have three  attention heads  and each attention head computes um  an equivalent expression like the one  you see here  but each attention head use different  sets of  weights and in the end  the features from all the attention  heads are averaged  to obtain the final representation  and here i included a slide to explain  how the attention mechanism works  but due to time concerns i will skip  this for now but if anyone  is interested to know more about it feel  free to ask me at the end of the  workshop  to wrap things up uh here's a short  summary on  the theory we've covered so far on gns  so we have seen that spatial approaches  are  more desirable for learning large graphs  due to the fact that they are more  computationally efficient  and they have better generality and  better flexibility  we also covered two of the popular  spatial frameworks  graph sage that uses  aggregator functions and you can  incorporate sampling  to assign importance to neighbors  while a graphic tension network use  multi-hat attention  for neighborhood importance  okay i'm about to move on to the  application part  so since graph has gained a lot of  attention in research  and applications there are a couple of  really well developed gnn packages for  python  my personal experience i've used pytorch  geometric  which is a graph deep learning extension  library for pytorch  and i have used a stellar graph that is  built on tensorflow and keras  both of these packages contain  a huge variety of graph models and a lot  of  common benchmark data sets for you to  explore  while i recommend both of them i found  stella  much easier to learn for beginners so in  the next 10 minutes  i will walk you through a node  classification example  using stellar graph and  this node classification task is  performed on  a citation network data set  so this citation network data set  consists of one citation  graph and this graph contains uh  roughly 3000 publications  and they're all classified into seven  subjects as such  this network consists of over 5000  links and each publication in the data  set  has an initial feature of  1400 and this feature is derived  from a dictionary depending on uh  if those words from dictionary are  contained in the publication  right so the goal of this node  classification task  is to classify the subject of the paper  given  its initial vocabulary features and its  citation connections  and then we would also like to explore  whether or not  incorporating graph structure can help  with node classification so we will  compare  a simple neural network as a baseline  model  with 